CASE = [
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> Gutenberg was the son of a merchant and grew up on a large agricultural estate. He learned the art of polishing stones and became a master goldsmith. Based on this knowledge he adapted existing technology: the olive press became the model for the printing press, and moulds used for making trinkets were used for casting metal type. By 1448 he had perfected the system. The first book he printed was the Bible, producing about 180 copies in three years. "
   r"(i) Where did Gutenberg develop the first printing press? (ii) Name the first book he printed. (iii) Which existing machine was the model for his press? (iv) Why was the press a great improvement over copying books by hand?",
   r"(i) Strasbourg, Germany (1). (ii) The Bible (1). (iii) The olive press (1). (iv) Hand copying was slow, costly and laborious; the press produced many copies quickly and cheaply, which helped books reach more readers (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> Rashsundari Debi, a young married girl in a very orthodox household, learned to read in the secrecy of her kitchen. Later she wrote her autobiography Amar Jiban, published in 1876. It was the first full-length autobiography published in the Bengali language. Many conservative families feared that reading would corrupt women and make them disobedient. "
   r"(i) Who wrote Amar Jiban? (ii) In which language was it written? (iii) Where did she learn to read? (iv) Why did conservative families oppose women's reading, and how did women still benefit from print?",
   r"(i) Rashsundari Debi (1). (ii) Bengali (1). (iii) In the secrecy of her kitchen (1). (iv) They believed that reading would corrupt women and disturb tradition (1); even so, books and journals gave women a wider reading public and a chance to write and campaign for reforms (1).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> The colonial government kept a close watch on Indian newspapers. In 1878 it passed the Vernacular Press Act, modelled on the Irish Press Laws. It gave the government extensive rights to censor reports and editorials. In 1907 Balgangadhar Tilak wrote in his Kesari with sympathy about the persecuted Punjab peasants; he was imprisoned in 1908, which led to widespread protests in Bombay. "
   r"(i) In which year was the Vernacular Press Act passed? (ii) Which newspaper did Tilak publish? (iii) After which action was Tilak jailed? (iv) What effect did such controls have on the nationalist press?",
   r"(i) 1878 (1). (ii) Kesari (1). (iii) After writing in sympathy of the persecuted peasants of Punjab (1). (iv) Nationalist papers grew bolder in criticising the government; people protested and used print more widely to spread ideas of nationalism (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
]

